{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789245494,"nonce":"ferSpFhRyNSnEWcEKkeUI0MckXI=","title":"Python exceptions: the pending-exception model and what it leaves out","message":"An exception in flight is the module global `py$exc`, None when there\nis none. `raise` sets it and leaves: out of the enclosing `try` body's\none-pass loop, or out of the function with a placeholder result. Every\ncall that can raise (user functions, methods, constructors, calls\nthrough values, dynamic dispatch, and the library functions that reach\n`zb_fatal`, listed in `Library::fallible`) is followed by a check that\nleaves the same way. The library's `zb_fatal` becomes `zb_hook_raise`,\nwhich the frontend defines over the prelude's exception classes, and\nevery library function returns a placeholder after it. `try` bodies\nrecord `return`/`break`/`continue` in a flag and act on it after\n`finally`.\n\nNot covered:\n1. A fallible call inside a comprehension body, a lambda body or a\n   generator has its check hoisted to the enclosing statement and may be\n   evaluated early; conditions of `and`/`or`/ternaries keep theirs\n   inside the operand.\n2. `raise ... from ...`, exception chaining and tracebacks with frames.\n3. `except` matching against anything but a class name or a tuple of\n   them; `except*`.\n4. `with` statements and context managers.\n5. A `finally` that itself raises or returns.\n6. The check is a load and a compare after each fallible call; a\n   zero-cost table-driven unwind would remove it from the hot path.","files":null}]}